Planex antipattern_estimate — why Estimate is necessary
=========================================================
Shows: useState/signal cannot cleanly express time + uncertainty

[anti-pattern 1] Animation — time as first-class dimension
  React: useState(0) + useEffect + setTimeout + manual lerp = ~20 LOC glue
  Planex: px_estimate_animate(e, target, ms) — Estimate IS time-varying
  Sampled values: t=0 -> 0.00, t=250 -> 43.75, t=500 -> 75.00, t=1000 -> 100.00
  PASS — monotonic, endpoints exact

[anti-pattern 2] Uncertainty — confidence as first-class field
  React: useState(42) + separate useState(confidence) — manual pair
  Planex: px_estimate_new(value, confidence) — single concept
  Confidence changes independently: value=42, conf=0.8 -> 0.95
  PASS — confidence is first-class

[anti-pattern 3] Auto-derived state — no manual dep list
  React: useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b]) — manual dep list
  Planex: px_derived_new(sum, srcs, 2) — Relation tracks deps
  Initial: a=10 + b=20 = total=30
  After a=50: total=70 (auto-updated, no manual list)
  After b=100: total=150 (auto-updated again)
  PASS — derived auto-tracks sources via Relation

=== Summary ===
Mainstream state (useState/signal) requires THREE patches:
  1. useEffect + setTimeout for animation
  2. separate state for confidence
  3. useMemo + manual dep list for derived

Planex Estimate subsumes all three as first-class:
  1. px_estimate_animate (time is in the abstraction)
  2. px_estimate_new(value, confidence) (uncertainty is field)
  3. px_derived_new (Relation tracks deps automatically)

Estimate is NECESSARY: useState + patches is a strict subset.

=== Estimate anti-pattern test complete ===
